## Context

Core 3.0's authority, types and fidelity requirements can be read as satisfied by consistent
output schemas even when procedures independently define their meaning. This decision extends
ADR-0002's repository-owned standard; its review cadence and disposition policy remain in force.

## Options

1. **Add only a principle paragraph.** Leaves review questions and agent summaries incomplete.
2. **Add another foundation or a modeling framework.** Duplicates assessment or prescribes machinery.
3. **Strengthen the existing foundations and judgments** (chosen). One governing MUST, FP-04
   ownership and A2 acceptance preserve the other foundations and freedom of implementation.

## Decision

- Adopt core/template 3.1{% if with_ci_profile %} and code-intelligence guidance 1.2{% endif %}; retain existing IDs and historical review versions.
- An explicit, coherent domain model must govern behavior wherever domain meaning is established
  or interpreted. FP-04/A2 assess both model adequacy and authoritative realization. A deferral
  cannot waive the requirement for supported behavior, even when outputs are currently correct.
- Model operations as well as concepts; own invariants, contextual bindings and representation
  contracts. Ordinary types, relations and domain functions suffice; no universal ontology,
  custom language, registry or serialization of every operation is required.
- Reviews trace phenomenon → authoritative concept or operation → implementation → consumer
  → expected change. Classify changes as instances, bindings, compositions, policies, domain
  concepts or mechanisms. Keep other architectural and fidelity judgments independent.
- The template, reviewer, process skills and agent instructions describe the same requirement.
  Library exploration and capability skills are unchanged; no new gate, hook or artifact type.

## Consequences

This is Implemented documentation policy, not a claim of product conformance. Improved change
locality remains Proposed until supported by actual changes. A mechanism replacement preserves
the domain contract or exposes incompatibility; no promise makes every replacement inexpensive.
Historical reviews retain their recorded scope and standard version.
